# curvescatter

- Label: Scatter on Curves
- Category: Copernicus (`Cop`), Houdini 22.0.368
- Inputs: 0 to 12
- Parameters: 195 (exact, read from Houdini by hython)
- Used by the height graph: no

## Feature summary (web, SideFX reference)

- Source: https://www.sidefx.com/docs/houdini/nodes/cop/curvescatter.html
- Summary (EN): Scatters one or more input stamps along an input curve, with randomisation of placement, scale, angle, stretch, colour and alpha. Titled Scatter on Curves in Houdini 22.0.
- Résumé (FR) : Répartit des tampons le long d'une courbe, avec des variations de position, d'échelle, d'angle, d'étirement, de couleur et d'alpha.

- Inputs meaning: ["size_ref: sets output image size and metadata", "curve: curve to scatter along (required)", "density: rejection-sampling layer used with Distribution set to Scatter", "direction: controls stamp angle direction", "angle: rotates stamps (0 = 0 degrees, 1 = 360 degrees)", "scale: multiplies uniform stamp scale", "stretch: multiplies non-uniform stamp scale", "color: multiplies stamp colour", "alpha: multiplies stamp alpha", "sort: depth-based sorting", "select: chooses stamps when Selection Mode is Layer Select", "stamps: up to 8 stamps; a default star is used if unwired"]

- Key parameters: ["Distribution: Distance (equal spacing), Scatter (random via density or Max Count), By Count", "Max Count, Relaxation, Scatter Seed", "Treat Input Curve As: Straight, Subdivision or Interpolating", "Scale, Angle, Stretch: base values with variance or min/max randomisation and layer masks", "Mirror Stamps: mirrors across the curve tangent", "Blend Method: Depth-Sorted Over, Add, Multiply, Maximum and others", "Stamp Color / Stamp Alpha: Uniform, Variance, Random from Ramp, or Ramp along Curve", "Selection Mode: Cycle, Random, Layer Select, Limit Selection", "Wrap Mode, Tile Size"]

- Caveats: ["The curve input must be wired or the node does not work.", "Density Scale has no effect when Max Count is on.", "Mirrored angles may not stay symmetric under Treat Independently randomisation.", "Depth-Sorted Over is expensive because it sorts."]

